hi there steve kaufman here  i just came back from my uh 10-day trip  to france uh i was there selling  cedar shakes and shingles  these are consumed in france primarily  in the mountains in the savoia area  and so i was  in makko the first night then we were up  in bonnville and ansi in that area in  the savoia and we ended up down in  valance because we have a customer in  that area and i had a wonderful meal and  of course i was speaking french every  day and having a great time really  enjoying myself  and i didn't have time to study any  korean of course  one interesting thing that happened to  me  i was in paris and there was a meetup of  the polyglot group  uh they advertised themselves on the  internet polyglot.com i think  uh vance who is the founder or at least  the organizer he was there i had a i  have communicated with him but this is  my first chance to meet with him  and there are  up to a hundred people who once or twice  a week gather  to speak different languages uh when i  was last in paris i also happened to be  there  at a time when they had one of their  meetings and at that time  the discussion while we had different  tables with flags on the tables and so  you could float from a  chinese table to a spanish table to a  german table and so forth english table  this time most of the people seem to be  interested in learning english  uh varian you know obviously very  enthusiastic people most of them were  french but there were also people there  from  japan  from  jordan from  spain  trying to remember here  china  u.s  different people exchanging languages  but very heavily oriented towards  english  and  people who very much wanted to improve  their english very um  motivated people  um but then i came home and of course i  bought some books in french and i'm  reading french i bought a book on  a biography of francois pomier who was  the king in the in the 1500s very much  involved with introducing the  renaissance to france  um  i read a few other books in french  one on the economy of happiness which  was kind of interesting  um  but then you know i i have this korean  lurking in the background that i want to  get back to  but then i'll uh have to write something  in russian on our forum at link and i  struggle and to use it and i refer to  google translate to help me  get going on this thing and and so i  really would like to spend more time on  my russian not to mention my check  but even at this meet up speaking in  german or spanish i realize that there  are gaps in my knowledge of these  languages  so the issue always is you know how much  you know i even and i was in a bookstore  in london and  because i visited in london my son and  his family lived there and i always  liked going to the bookstores there and  i was toying with getting  looking at what they're doing at any  rate in arabic and of course the first  obstacle in arabic is the writing system  which i would have to get a handle on i  think before i started studying arabic  at linc  but then i said geez if i take on arabic  i haven't even gotten as far as i want  to get in korean  i still want to improve my russian and  czech not to mention all my other  languages with every language you take  on  it's more and more difficult  to take any of your other languages up  to a higher level which i would also  like to do there's only only so many  hours of the day and i typically only  have an hour or so because i also like  to read on different subjects and so  forth so the question is you know how  much how much do you want to be a  perfectionist now obviously someone who  needs say english for their work  they can't afford to be a dilettante i'm  more of a dilettante  and so i've kind of decided i'm not  going to allow myself to be  to feel pressure  uh i'll speak those languages as well as  i speak them period and there will  always be people who will criticize me  and say that my whatever language is no  good and they do that on youtube  regularly so that's fine but as i often  say you know language is for ourselves  so  i derive tremendous enjoyment from all  the languages that i speak  uh from being able to understand  from being able to communicate albeit  with mistakes so i'm happy i i achieve  you know uh what i want to achieve i get  back what i put in  um  so i guess and i think that's good  enough so for those of us who study more  than one language  uh or who are doing it as a hobby we  shouldn't feel pressure i think we  shouldn't feel this pressure to be  perfect nail down the grammar make sure  we understand everything and i often get  emails from people or comments here at  youtube i've been studying such and such  a language for so long and i still can't  understand the movies or i still make  mistakes or have trouble expressing  myself and i think we shouldn't  allow ourselves to be sort of hounded by  by this inability to be as good as we  would like to be that's always going to  be there you always have this feeling as  i have that i in all my languages that i  could be better and if i had the time  then i would devote myself to trying to  get better i mean japanese i'd like to  get reading japanese literature and  expand my vocabulary in that direction  and  there's no end you know i've got 12  languages that i claim i speak and  korean would be the 13th and in every  one of them i could improve if i had  more time to spend on listening and  reading and  noticing and gathering phrases and so  forth  however what do you do so as long as  you're just a dilettante like me you're  doing it for fun you can quite  comfortably say i don't care what people  say i'm happy  the languages work for me for what i  want to do with them and i don't allow  myself to get frustrated by  the obvious shortcomings in my  ability to use these languages but what  about if you need it for work there's no  question that if you are  professional let's say if you're an  immigrant to canada  you're an accountant you're an engineer  you have to speak english and write  english very well and i know many  non-native speakers who write  better than the average canadian in  english  and who speak although with an accent  but who express themselves better more  eloquently than the average native  speaking english-speaking canadian so  that can be achieved so for those people  what do they need to do well obviously  they need to concentrate and if i  were to move to a country let's say you  know czech republic russia spain  anywhere where i had to do business in  that language  that was the main language of business  that i was sending out letters or emails  then i would totally focus on that  language and i would  read a lot in that language i would you  know obviously save words and phrases  review these words and phrases i would  probably get a teacher to  correct my writing again not because i'm  going to immediately improve after the  correction but because it's it's just  helps me to to focus in on areas of  weakness and and look for those things  when i'm reading and speaking and and  so i and i believe that if i were if i  had the luxury of focusing on spanish or  chinese or czech that i could take it up  to a very very high level so i  deliberately  choose  not to do that because i'm interested in  exploring other languages  but there's a limit you know maybe  arabic and turkish  but then i may just you know stop there  or maybe just kind of explore some of  the others much the way moses does moses  mccormick he explores a variety of  languages it's all good it's all a  matter of what we want to do  you know it's a matter of whether you  want to be an expert in a small number  or you want to travel and see and  experience a variety of languages so  those are all valid  goals  so i guess my conclusion is that  how perfect we need to be depends on  your goals if you are working in a  language where you need the language  professionally you have to devote  yourself to becoming as good as you can  be  you have to become very attentive you  have to  see  and notice  uh excellent use of the language in  others when you're reading and listening  and you have to therefore see and notice  when you yourself are not using the  language as effectively as you could and  this is not a matter of accent i don't  think accent really matters i've said it  before i deal with people here in canada  my father as i mentioned who  handled himself very well here in  english always had a strong accent  the accent i don't think is a big issue  but you can  train yourself to notice what is good  usage effective efficient use of the  language by the natives and you can get  very close to imitating that yourself if  you focus on that language but if your  desire is to know a number of languages  because you like to travel you like to  read in different languages watch movies  in different languages  be able to communicate with people that  you come across in your travels then  don't get upset because you don't  achieve perfection in three or four  different languages  just  again as i always say pat yourself on  the back for what you are able to do  derive enjoyment from it enjoy it  and don't  allow yourself  to be feel frustrated  and and i say that and yet and yet i  must admit that at times it bothers me  that i'm not better in some of the  languages  that i claim to speak but i just don't  have time  to achieve a higher level in some of  those if i also want to have fine time  to learn new languages not to mention  reading leading the rest of my life so  how perfect do we need to be it's  entirely up to us it depends on what our  goals are but if we need to be very good  in the language we can do it as long as  we don't have unrealistic expectations  when it comes to accents  and we accept the fact that we will make  the odd mistake thanks for listening bye  for now

